---
import CopyCode from "../../../components/CopyCode.astro";
import DocsLayout from "../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";

const toc = [
  { id: "install", label: "Install" },
  { id: "scaffold", label: "Scaffold a Python build" },
  { id: "first-build", label: "Your first build" },
  { id: "run", label: "Running it" },
  { id: "existing", label: "Into an existing project" },
  { id: "ci", label: "On CI" },
  { id: "engine", label: "The engine pin" },
  { id: "differences", label: "What differs from TypeScript" },
  { id: "next", label: "Next steps" },
];

const installSnippet = `brew install zuke-build/tap/zuke   # macOS, Linux
choco install zuke                 # Windows`;

const setupSnippet = `zuke setup --sdk python   # build.py, zuke.json, the launchers
zuke                      # run the default target`;

const starterSnippet = `# /// script
# requires-python = ">=3.12"
# dependencies = ["zuke"]
# ///
"""Your project's build. Run a target with ./zuke <target>."""

from zuke import Build, run, target


class App(Build):
    """The targets of this project."""

    hello = (
        target()
        .description("A sample target, replace me with real work")
        .executes(lambda ctx: ctx.log("Hello from Zuke!"))
    )

    # Convention: default runs when no target is named.
    default = target().description("Default target").depends_on(hello)


run(App)`;

const zukeJsonSnippet = `{
  "name": "App",
  "sdk": { "command": ["uv", "run", "build.py"] },
  "engine": "1.4.0"
}`;

const buildSnippet = `from zuke import Build, ci, parameter, run, target
from zuke.tools.docker import DockerTasks


class App(Build):
    tag = parameter("image tag").default("dev")
    pipeline = ci().path(".github/workflows/ci.yml")

    lint = target().description("ruff").executes(lambda ctx: ctx.sh("ruff", "check", "."))
    test = target().depends_on(lint).executes(lambda ctx: ctx.sh("pytest", "-q"))
    image = (
        target()
        .depends_on(test)
        .executes(
            lambda ctx: DockerTasks.build(
                ctx, lambda s: s.tag(f"app:{tag.value}").file("Dockerfile")
            )
        )
    )


run(App)`;

const runSnippet = `zuke image                 # lint, test, then image — once each, in order
zuke image --tag 1.4.0     # parameters are flags (or TAG in the environment)
zuke --list                # every target, its description and dependencies
zuke graph --output=html   # the dependency graph, interactive
zuke generate-ci           # write the workflow the ci() field declares
zuke mcp                   # serve the build to an agent as typed tools`;

const existingSnippet = `uv add --dev zuke   # or: pip install zuke`;

const ciSnippet = `./zuke ci       # Linux and macOS: downloads the pinned zuke on first use, then runs it
./zuke.ps1 ci   # Windows`;

const upgradeSnippet = `zuke upgrade --engine            # move the launchers and the pin to the latest zuke
zuke upgrade --engine 2.4.0      # or to an exact zuke release
zuke upgrade --engine --dry-run  # list what it would rewrite`;
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Python quickstart"
  description="Write a Zuke build in typed Python: install the CLI, scaffold build.py with zuke setup --sdk python, and run your first targets on the Zuke engine."
  toc={toc}
>
  <p class="lead">
    A Zuke build in Python is a class whose fields are targets, with
    dependencies the type checker sees and a typed facade for every tool. The
    Zuke <a href="/docs/engine">engine</a> runs it — scheduling, every command,
    caching, locks, resume, CI generation and the MCP server — exactly as it
    runs a TypeScript build. This page gets you from nothing to a green build.
  </p>

  <h2 id="install">Install</h2>
  <p>
    You need <a href="https://docs.astral.sh/uv/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">uv</a>,
    and the <code>zuke</code> command to drive a build from your shell. It is
    a standalone binary that carries the engine and every tool wrapper, so a
    Python build needs <strong>no Deno, anywhere</strong>:
  </p>
  <CopyCode code={installSnippet} lang="bash" multi />
  <p>
    The Python package is <a href="https://pypi.org/project/zuke/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><code>zuke</code> on PyPI</a>.
    It needs Python 3.12 or newer and has no runtime dependencies. A scaffolded
    build installs it by itself, so there is nothing to <code>pip install</code> yet.
    A machine without <code>zuke</code>, such as a CI runner, needs only the
    project's launchers: they download the same binary (see <a href="#ci">On CI</a>).
  </p>

  <h2 id="scaffold">Scaffold a Python build</h2>
  <p>In your project:</p>
  <CopyCode code={setupSnippet} lang="bash" multi />
  <p>
    <code>zuke setup --sdk python</code> writes a starter <code>build.py</code>,
    a <code>zuke.json</code>, the <code>./zuke</code> and <code>zuke.ps1</code>
    launchers and a <code>.gitignore</code>, and no <code>deno.json</code>. The
    starter carries inline script metadata, so <code>uv run build.py</code>
    installs the <code>zuke</code> package with no project file of its own:
  </p>
  <CopyCode code={starterSnippet} lang="python" />
  <p>
    <code>zuke.json</code> names the command that starts your build and the
    exact engine release that runs it:
  </p>
  <CopyCode code={zukeJsonSnippet} lang="json" />
  <p>
    The launchers pin one <code>zuke</code> release: the one that ran
    <code>setup</code>, or the one <code>--zuke &lt;version&gt;</code> names.
    <code>zuke.json</code> pins the engine inside it.
    <code>--no-bootstrap</code> writes launchers that require <code>zuke</code>
    on <code>PATH</code> and fail closed without it, instead of downloading it,
    and <code>--mcp</code> registers the build's MCP server for your agent as
    <code>zuke mcp</code>. See the <a href="/docs/cli#project">CLI reference</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="first-build">Your first build</h2>
  <p>Replace the sample target with real work:</p>
  <CopyCode code={buildSnippet} lang="python" />
  <ul>
    <li><code>depends_on(lint)</code> is a reference to the sibling field, not a string: pyright sees it, a rename moves every use, and a typo fails before anything runs. A target may only depend on fields declared <strong>above</strong> it.</li>
    <li><code>ctx.sh(...)</code> runs a command through the engine — argv only, no shell — and raises <code>CommandError</code> on a non-zero exit.</li>
    <li><code>DockerTasks.build(ctx, lambda s: ...)</code> is the typed Docker wrapper, configured with a settings lambda exactly like <code>DockerTasks.build((s) =&gt; ...)</code> in TypeScript.</li>
    <li><code>tag.value</code> is the parameter's resolved value, set by the engine before any body runs.</li>
    <li><code>run(App)</code> makes the file a build; it does nothing when the file is imported.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="run">Running it</h2>
  <p>
    With the CLI installed, drive the build from anywhere inside the project,
    exactly as a TypeScript one:
  </p>
  <CopyCode code={runSnippet} lang="bash" multi />
  <p>
    <code>zuke --help</code> lists the build's live targets and parameters, and
    <code>zuke --version</code> names the <code>zuke.json</code> and the engine
    release it runs.
  </p>

  <h2 id="existing">Into an existing project</h2>
  <p>
    A project with its own <code>pyproject.toml</code> can add the package as a
    dev dependency instead:
  </p>
  <CopyCode code={existingSnippet} lang="bash" />
  <p>
    Then delete the <code># /// script</code> block from <code>build.py</code>:
    uv runs a script that carries that block in an environment of its own, and
    one without it in your project's environment, beside your own tools. The
    scaffolded <code>sdk.command</code>, <code>["uv", "run", "build.py"]</code>,
    stays as it is.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ci">On CI</h2>
  <p>
    A CI runner has no <code>zuke</code> command, so it runs the committed
    launchers. They pin one <code>zuke</code> release and the SHA-256 of each
    platform's archive. On first use they download it from the GitHub release,
    verify it against the pin, keep it under <code>~/.zuke</code>
    (<code>ZUKE_INSTALL</code>) and run it. <code>ZUKE_RELEASES_URL</code>
    names a mirror, whose download is verified the same way. uv provides the
    Python side, and a CI job needs nothing else.
  </p>
  <CopyCode code={ciSnippet} lang="bash" multi />
  <p>
    Better still, let the build write its own pipeline: the <code>ci()</code>
    field above becomes <code>.github/workflows/ci.yml</code> with
    <code>zuke generate-ci</code>, and <code>zuke generate-ci --check</code>
    fails when the committed file drifts. See
    <a href="/docs/python/authoring#ci">CI files</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="engine">The engine pin</h2>
  <p>
    <code>zuke.json</code>'s <code>engine</code> is an exact release, never a
    range, so the engine cannot change between two runs. Each <code>zuke</code>
    release carries one engine, so the launchers and the pin move together,
    deliberately, and never by hand:
  </p>
  <CopyCode code={upgradeSnippet} lang="bash" multi />
  <p>
    It rewrites <code>./zuke</code> and <code>zuke.ps1</code> to pin that
    release and its archives' digests, keeping their variant, and
    <code>zuke.json</code>'s <code>engine</code> to the engine inside it. It
    also moves a project still on the old Deno launchers to the binary ones.
  </p>

  <h2 id="differences">What differs from TypeScript</h2>
  <p>The model, the CLI and every engine feature are the same. The spelling is Python's:</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>TypeScript</th><th>Python</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>class CI extends Build</code></td><td><code>class CI(Build)</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>.dependsOn(this.lint)</code></td><td><code>.depends_on(lint)</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>.executes(async () =&gt; ...)</code></td><td><code>.executes(lambda ctx: ...)</code> or an <code>async def body(ctx)</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>await $`pytest -q`</code></td><td><code>await ctx.sh("pytest", "-q")</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>import {"{ DockerTasks }"} from "@zuke/docker"</code></td><td><code>from zuke.tools.docker import DockerTasks</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>DockerTasks.build((s) =&gt; s.tag("a"))</code></td><td><code>DockerTasks.build(ctx, lambda s: s.tag("a"))</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>cicd(&#123; provider: "github" &#125;)</code></td><td><code>ci().provider("github")</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>await run(CI)</code></td><td><code>run(CI)</code></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    <code>zuke add</code>, <code>remove</code>, <code>cache</code> and
    <code>relock</code> manage a project's own Deno code, so they refuse a
    Python build: its tool wrappers come with the SDK, as <code>zuke.tools</code>.
  </p>

